  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe 500MB ridge over the central CONUS will shift east as \nnorthwest flow sets up over the Great Lakes and into the Upper\nOhio Valley. This will set up a series of shortwaves to travel\nsoutheast over the region the next few days. The timing of the\nshortwaves over the next few days will be the main issue as hi-\nres models and ensembles are not handling the setup very well \nin the timing and placement. \n\nThere stands to be some convection in the morning just after\ndawn but the hi-res CAMs have been very inconsistent with the\ndevelopment. This same case goes for convection later in the\nday. Some runs of the HRRR have showed nothing all day and some\nshow strong convection crossing the north. The consensus does\nthrow severe convection over the northeastern Counties by the\n21Z to 02Z timeframe depending on what run on what model shows\nit. Winds remains a threat but the hail risk increases as well\nas a small tornado chance.  \n\nThe next threat falls into Thursday as the front begins to sag\nsouth and bringing more the CWA into the storm track/main flow.\nSufficient heating and some shear remaining will make for \nanother day of severe convection. Timing a couple days out puts \nthe threat during the evening timeframe. As the front continues \nto sag south, the very southern extent of the forecast area may \nsee some weak convection on Friday as well. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA 500mb ridge extending over the Great Lakes and Ohio River\nValley will help bring in the warmer temperatures through\nThursday. The heat should peak Wednesday with highs in the \nupper 80s to lower 90s for most areas. This combined with higher \nhumidity is likely to result in heat index values in the upper\n90s to 100 Wednesday. A Heat Advisory was issued for eastern\nOhio, most of northern West Virginia, and southwestern PA for\nareas that are likely to reach heat indices of 100F.\n\nHeat is forecast to linger into Thursday as the 500mb ridge\nweakens and moves southeastward. Temperatures in the upper 80s\nand lower 90s with heat indices in the upper 90s to low 100s. A\ntrough moving into the area behind the ridge will bring cloud \ncover and the chance for showers/storms which could inhibit \ndaytime heating. Timing is uncertain in that regard so the Heat\nAdvisory was extended into Thursday though, leaving out more\nnorthern counties.",
